{
  "dom_id": "step-q143318",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "The marginal mass functions of the independent discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ are shown below. Given that the corresponding joint probability mass function is $f(x,y),$ find $f(6,5).$\n\n| | $x$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ | $0.1$ | $0.4$ | |  | | $y$ | $1$ | $3$ | $5$ | $7$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ | $0.25$ | $0.6$ | |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ | $0.1$ | $0.4$ |\n| $y$ | $1$ | $3$ | $5$ | $7$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ | $0.25$ | $0.6$ |",
  "worked_solution": "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nTherefore, since our discrete random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(6,5) & ={f}_{X}(6)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(5) \\\\  & =0.1\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =0.025.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
